{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["Ibn Ezra said that the reason for <b>“and at Taberah and at Massah”</b> is that since at Taberah the people were like complainers, testing HaShem with pretexts, He reminded them: you were provoking HaShem by the trial with which you tried Him at Taberah, just as you had done once before at Massah, and you did not take instruction. And this is a fine interpretation. Now the sin of testing HaShem is a grave sin and a great guilt, as He warned against it (Deuteronomy 6:16): “You shall not test YHWH your Elohim, as you tested Him at Massah” — therefore He brought it back to them here. And so He mentioned once again, in brief, the sin of the spies because of the greatness of the iniquity, for in it you were consummate rebels against YHWH. And He said, “from the day I knew you,” to allude to their complaints before the giving of the Torah."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:47:20.922685146Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}